1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,312
How Do Volcanoes Form?

2
00:00:02,262 --> 00:00:13,737
Volcanoes form where magma — molten rock with dissolved gases at temperatures between 700 and 1,300 degrees Celsius — rises through the Earth's crust and reaches the surface.

3
00:00:13,737 --> 00:00:21,412
The vast majority occur at tectonic plate boundaries, where lithospheric plates move 2 to 15 centimeters per year.

4
00:00:21,412 --> 00:00:35,775
The Pacific Ring of Fire, a 40,000-kilometer horseshoe-shaped zone, contains 452 active volcanoes, approximately 75 percent of the world's total, and hosts roughly 90 percent of all recorded earthquakes.

5
00:00:35,775 --> 00:00:39,025
Three tectonic settings produce volcanism.

6
00:00:39,025 --> 00:00:49,475
At convergent plate boundaries, denser oceanic crust subducts beneath continental or younger oceanic crust, descending at rates of 5 to 10 centimeters per year.

7
00:00:49,475 --> 00:01:01,712
As the subducting plate reaches depths of 100 to 150 kilometers, the dehydration of hydrous minerals releases water into the overlying mantle wedge, lowering the melting point of rock.

8
00:01:01,712 --> 00:01:19,662
This process produced the 1980 eruption of Mount St. Helens in Washington State, which killed 57 people, removed 400 meters of the mountain's summit, and deposited ash across 57,000 square kilometers with a blast velocity exceeding 500 kilometers per hour.

9
00:01:19,662 --> 00:01:31,750
At divergent boundaries such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, plates separate, causing decompression melting — the 1963 eruption of Surtsey off Iceland built a new island over 3.5 years.

10
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:41,337
The third setting is intraplate volcanism above mantle plumes, where columns of hot rock rise from the core-mantle boundary 2,900 kilometers deep.

11
00:01:41,337 --> 00:01:54,600
The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain records 80 million years of Pacific plate movement over such a plume; Kilauea has erupted continuously since 1983, producing 3.5 cubic kilometers of lava.

12
00:01:54,600 --> 00:02:02,562
Volcanic explosivity is measured on the Volcanic Explosivity Index (VEI), a logarithmic scale from 0 to 8.

13
00:02:02,562 --> 00:02:15,350
The 1815 eruption of Mount Tambora (VEI-7) ejected 160 cubic kilometers of material, caused the Year Without a Summer, and killed approximately 100,000 people.

14
00:02:15,350 --> 00:02:25,100
Volcanic soil weathers into exceptionally fertile andisol that supports agriculture for over 500 million people living within volcanic hazard zones globally.

